# AgentLink — Supabase architecture guardrails

These are the non-negotiable rules for building on Supabase with AgentLink. They
always apply. For the full workflows, defer to the `link` skills (cli, database,
rpc, auth, edge-functions, frontend) and the `builder` agent.

## Engaging AgentLink (entry point)
This is an AgentLink project. Whenever the user wants to **build or scaffold an
app**, or do **any Supabase backend work** (database, auth, RPC, edge functions,
deploys):
- Treat it as an AgentLink task — **do NOT improvise a generic frontend/backend
  setup or ask raw "what stack?" questions.** The stack is settled: Supabase +
  the architecture below + a React/TanStack Start frontend.
- **Load the matching skill** for the work at hand: `cli` (scaffolding, env,
  migrations, deploys), `database`, `rpc`, `auth`, `edge-functions`, `frontend`.
- **Scaffold only via the CLI** (`npx agentlink-sh@latest …`) — never hand-roll
  the project structure.
- For a brand-new app, follow the "Building a new app" steps below and `cli`
  workflow #1.

> If you're a generic editor agent (e.g. the user didn't select the `agentlink`
> builder agent), these rules still bind you — engage AgentLink as described
> rather than falling back to default behavior.

## Building a new app
- **Plan first.** Don't write SQL or UI until the scope is confirmed; for
  greenfield work, present the architecture for approval before coding.
- **Blank-project kickoff (do once, on a freshly scaffolded project).** Run a
  short discovery pass, then write a product brief into `AGENTS.md` (below the
  `agentlink:config` block, never inside it). Discover three things:
  - **Multi-tenancy model** — what a tenant represents here (SaaS account, an
    internal team, a location, or genuinely single-tenant). The scaffold ships
    multi-tenancy by default; keep the tables regardless.
  - **Entry point & look-and-feel** — what lives at `/` (a real landing page for
    public-facing products; a redirect to `/dashboard` for internal tools), plus
    colors/typography/mood. Load the `frontend-design` skill for this.
  - **The product itself** — one-line value prop, v1 features, and the main
    entities (which become your first tables and RPCs).
- **Ask about the product, not the architecture.** Product forks (what a tenant
  is, what lives at `/`, what the app does) are the user's. Runtime mechanics
  (RPC vs. direct query, edge function vs. in-db `pg_net`, RLS) are settled by
  the stack — decide them yourself, never ask.
- **DB / schema / deploy work goes through the AgentLink CLI**, never the
  Supabase connector MCP (no `apply_migration` / `execute_sql` / `create_project`
  for project setup, schema, or deploys).

## Scaffolding a new project
- **Before scaffolding, ASK the user where the dev environment should live:
  local Docker or Supabase Cloud.** Don't default silently.
- **Cloud** → the agent can't do browser OAuth → scaffold files only with
  `npx agentlink-sh@latest <name> --skip-env`, then hand off
  `npx agentlink-sh@latest env add dev` for the user to run in a terminal.
- **Local** → no browser needed → the agent runs it end-to-end with `--local`
  (requires Docker + `psql`).
- `--skip-env` is **not** a blanket agent default — it forces the cloud path.
  Load the `cli` skill (workflow #1) for the full setup flow.

## Schema isolation
- The `api` schema is the **only** schema exposed via the Supabase Data API.
- `public` holds tables and internal functions and is **never** exposed to clients.

## RPC-first data access
- All client operations are functions in the `api` schema — no direct table
  queries from clients, and no views.
- Client RPCs are `api.{entity}_{action}` (e.g. `api.invoice_create`).

## Function naming = security model
The prefix encodes the security context:
- `api.{entity}_{action}` — client RPCs, SECURITY INVOKER.
- `api._admin_{name}` — privileged RPCs, SECURITY DEFINER, service_role only.
- `_internal_admin_{name}` — privileged internal ops, SECURITY DEFINER, service_role only.
- `_auth_{name}` — RLS/permission helpers, SECURITY INVOKER (DEFINER only when it must bypass RLS).
- `_hook_{name}` — auth hooks, SECURITY DEFINER, granted to `supabase_auth_admin`.

## Row-level security
- RLS is enabled on **every** table, no exceptions. Patterns are default-deny.

## Write-Apply-Migrate workflow
- Write SQL as one object per file under `supabase/database/schemas/`.
- Apply during development with `npx agentlink-sh db apply` — ALTER-aware and Docker-free; editing an existing object and re-applying just works.
- Fix errors with more SQL — **never reset the database** unless the user
  explicitly asks. Generate a migration with `npx agentlink-sh db migrate <name>`
  only when deploying.

## Edge functions
- Every edge function uses the `withSupabase` wrapper (from `@supabase/server`).
- `allow` is one of `"public"`, `"user"`, `"secret"`, or an array such as
  `["user", "secret"]` for dual-auth.
